Tuples and Sets in Python (In-Depth Notes)
1. Tuples in Python
Definition
A tuple is an ordered collection of items. It is immutable, meaning once created, its values cannot be changed.
Features
Ordered
Immutable (cannot modify)
Allows duplicate values
Can store different data types
Faster than lists
Written using parentheses ()
Syntax
my_tuple = (10, 20, 30, 40)
print(my_tuple)
Output
(10, 20, 30, 40)
Creating Tuples
Empty Tuple
t = ()
print(t)
Single Element Tuple
A comma is required.
t = (5,)
print(t)
Multiple Elements
t = (1, 2, 3, 4)
print(t)
Mixed Data Types
t = (10, "Python", 3.14, True)
print(t)
Accessing Tuple Elements
Indexing
t = ("Apple", "Banana", "Orange")

print(t[0])
print(t[1])
print(t[2])
Output
Apple
Banana
Orange
Negative Indexing
print(t[-1])
print(t[-2])
Output
Orange
Banana
Tuple Slicing
t = (10, 20, 30, 40, 50)

print(t[1:4])
print(t[:3])
print(t[2:])
Output
(20, 30, 40)
(10, 20, 30)
(30, 40, 50)
Tuple Operations
Concatenation
a = (1, 2)
b = (3, 4)

print(a + b)
Output
(1, 2, 3, 4)
Repetition
t = (5,)

print(t * 4)
Output
(5, 5, 5, 5)
Tuple Methods
count()
Counts occurrences.
t = (1, 2, 3, 2, 2)

print(t.count(2))
Output
3
index()
Returns the index of the first occurrence.
t = (10, 20, 30)

print(t.index(20))
Output
1
Built-in Functions
t = (10, 20, 30)

print(len(t))
print(max(t))
print(min(t))
print(sum(t))
Output
3
30
10
60
Tuple Packing
student = ("Preethi", 20, "BCA")
print(student)
Output
('Preethi', 20, 'BCA')
Tuple Unpacking
name, age, course = ("Preethi", 20, "BCA")

print(name)
print(age)
print(course)
Output
Preethi
20
BCA
Advantages of Tuples
Faster than lists
Uses less memory
Safe because data cannot change
Can be used as dictionary keys
Disadvantages
Cannot modify elements
Cannot add or remove items directly
2. Sets in Python
Definition
A set is an unordered collection of unique elements.
Features
Unordered
Mutable
No duplicate values
Does not support indexing
Written using curly braces {}
Creating Sets
s = {10, 20, 30}

print(s)
Empty Set
s = set()
print(s)
Duplicate Values
s = {1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4}

print(s)
Output
{1, 2, 3, 4}
Adding Elements
add()
s = {10, 20}

s.add(30)

print(s)
update()
s = {1, 2}

s.update([3, 4, 5])

print(s)
Removing Elements
remove()
s = {10, 20, 30}

s.remove(20)

print(s)
discard()
s = {10, 20}

s.discard(30)

print(s)
(No error if the element is absent.)
pop()
s = {1, 2, 3}

print(s.pop())
print(s)
(Removes a random element.)
clear()
s = {1, 2, 3}

s.clear()

print(s)
Output
set()
Set Operations
Union
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {3, 4, 5}

print(A | B)
print(A.union(B))
Output
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
Intersection
print(A & B)
print(A.intersection(B))
Output
{3}
Difference
print(A - B)
Output
{1, 2}
Symmetric Difference
print(A ^ B)
Output
{1, 2, 4, 5}
Membership Operators
s = {10, 20, 30}

print(20 in s)
print(50 in s)
Output
True
False
Set Methods
s = {1, 2, 3}

print(len(s))
print(max(s))
print(min(s))
print(sum(s))
Output
3
3
1
6
Frozen Set
A frozenset is an immutable version of a set.
fs = frozenset([1, 2, 3])

print(fs)
Output
frozenset({1, 2, 3})
Advantages of Sets
Removes duplicate values automatically
Very fast searching
Supports mathematical set operations
Efficient for membership testing
Disadvantages of Sets
Unordered
No indexing or slicing
Duplicate values are not allowed
